\begin_layout Section
Methodology: The Minimal Decoupling Model (MDM)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We define the economy as a dynamical system governed by the interplay between capital accumulation, technological diffusion, and labor participation. The system state vector is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $x = [K_H, K_L, R, \theta, P, S]$
\end_inset

, representing High-Tech Capital, Low-Tech Capital, Automation Capital, Diffusion, Labor Participation, and the Accumulated Subsidy, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Production and Labor Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Output is generated via two sectors. The High-Tech sector (
\begin_inset Formula $Y_H$
\end_inset

) utilizes a Constant Elasticity of Substitution (CES) production function, allowing for substitutability between cognitive labor (
\begin_inset Formula $L_H$
\end_inset

) and automation (
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Y_H = A_H K_H^\alpha \left[ (1-\mu)L_H^\rho + \mu (\psi(\theta) R)^\rho \right]^{\frac{1}{\rho}}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho = (\sigma-1)/\sigma$
\end_inset

 determines the elasticity of substitution 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi(\theta)$
\end_inset

 represents the efficiency advantage of automation, which decays as technology diffuses (
\begin_inset Formula $\theta \to 1$
\end_inset

). The Low-Tech sector (
\begin_inset Formula $Y_L$
\end_inset

) follows a Cobb-Douglas specification:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
Y_L = A_L K_L^\beta L_L^{1-\beta}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Labor is allocated to equate the Marginal Product of Labor (MPL) across sectors, defining the market wage 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
w = \frac{\partial Y_H}{\partial L_H} = \frac{\partial Y_L}{\partial L_L}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Participation Dynamics and the Social Contract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Standard models assume inelastic labor supply. We model Participation (
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

) as a dynamic state driven by the gap between the effective wage (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\text{eff}}$
\end_inset

) and a reservation 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
"
\end_layout

\end_inset

survival
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
"
\end_layout

\end_inset

 wage (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{\text{bar}}$
\end_inset

). We employ a hyperbolic tangent function to model the saturation of the labor force and the friction of social change:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\dot{P} = 
\begin{cases} 
\eta P \tanh(\lambda (w_{\text{eff}} - w_{\text{bar}})) & \text{if } w_{\text{eff}} \geq w_{\text{bar}} \\
\delta_P P \tanh(\lambda (w_{\text{eff}} - w_{\text{bar}})) & \text{if } w_{\text{eff}} < w_{\text{bar}}
\end{cases}
 - \chi (\mu \psi)^\gamma (1-\theta) P
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final term represents 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
"
\end_layout

\end_inset

Skill Exclusion,
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
"
\end_layout

\end_inset

 where rapid technological shifts disenfranchise a fraction of the workforce regardless of wage levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Closed-Loop Control: The Thermostat
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To maintain participation at a target level (
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

), we introduce a Proportional-Integral (PI) Controller acting on the effective wage. The control input, the wage subsidy fraction 
\begin_inset Formula $S(t)$
\end_inset

, evolves to minimize the error 
\begin_inset Formula $e(t) = P_{\text{target}} - P(t)$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
S(t) = \text{sat}_{[0, S_{\text{max}}]} \left( K_p e(t) + K_i \int_{0}^{t} e(\tau) d\tau \right)
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The effective wage perceived by the workforce is thus augmented by the subsidy, funded by a fraction of total output 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
w_{\text{eff}} = w_{\text{market}} + \frac{S(t) \cdot Y}{P \cdot N}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Investment Friction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To reflect realistic constraints on capital scaling, we modify the standard accumulation equation 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{K} = I - \delta K$
\end_inset

 by introducing an adjustment cost factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

. The effective capital formation is given by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
\dot{K}_i = \frac{I_i}{1 + \phi \left( \frac{I_i}{K_i} \right)} - \delta_i K_i \quad \text{for } i \in \{H, L, R\}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As investment intensity (
\begin_inset Formula $I/K$
\end_inset

) rises, the marginal efficiency of investment declines, preventing instantaneous singularities in output growth.
\end_layout

